<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/advanced/breeding-and-gmos">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Breeding is <strong>applied evolution with a human judge</strong>. Every tool in this chapter —
    Punnett squares, selection indexes, CRISPR nucleases — does the same three things: creates genetic
    variation, lets the breeder choose among variants, and fixes the chosen alleles in a population.
    The continuum is unbroken. A farmer in highland Mexico who saved seed from the least shatter-prone
    teosinte plants was running the same algorithm a gene editor runs today; only the resolution of
    the choice has changed, from whole plants to whole genomes to single base pairs.
  </p>
  <p>
    That continuity matters because it reframes the argument people usually have about GMOs. Once you
    accept that conventional breeding already shuffles tens of thousands of genes with no molecular
    oversight, "which technique made this variety?" becomes a weaker question than "what does this
    variety do, and who controls its seed?" This chapter builds the biology first, then returns to
    that question with the evidence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="selection's history, the toolkit, the record, the governance fork" widget="equationStep" config={gmoCfg} />

  <h2>Monohybrid inheritance: the grammar of selection</h2>
  <p>
    Everything downstream rests on Mendel's insight that traits are determined by discrete particles —
    alleles — that segregate cleanly rather than blending. For a single locus with alleles A and a:
    a cross of two heterozygotes (Aa × Aa) produces F2 genotypes in the ratio 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa. If A
    is dominant, the phenotypes collapse to a visible 3 : 1. If dominance is incomplete, the 1 : 2 : 1
    shows straight through — which is why some ratios in seed catalogues look "non-Mendelian" but are
    simply unmasked.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> Suppose a wild relative of wheat carries a recessive gene
    (<em>aa</em>) for resistance to a fungal race your elite variety (<em>AA</em>) lacks. Cross them:
    every F1 plant is Aa — uniform, but susceptible, because resistance is hidden behind the dominant
    susceptibility allele. Self the F1 and screen the F2: three quarters are susceptible, one quarter
    resistant. If you also want to keep the elite background (99% of the elite genome, not just the
    resistance), each resistant F2 plant must be backcrossed to the elite parent, and because the
    trait is recessive you must progeny-test or genotype each generation to find carriers. Six to
    eight generations of that backcrossing was the standard route to a resistant variety before
    markers arrived — one reason disease resistance lags behind pathogen evolution.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "dominant" does not mean stronger, fitter, or more common in the
    population. Dominance describes what happens to the <em>phenotype</em> when both alleles are
    present; it says nothing about which allele spreads. Recessive alleles can be essential (most
    disease resistances breeders chase are recessive) and dominant lethal alleles cannot persist at
    all.
  </p>

  <h2>Selective breeding in animals and plants</h2>
  <p>
    Once inheritance is particulate, selection becomes quantitative. The breeder observes a trait,
    chooses the best animals or plants, and asks how much their offspring will improve. The answer is
    the <strong>breeder's equation</strong>, R = h² × S, where S (the selection differential) is how
    far the selected parents sit above the population average and h² is the narrow-sense heritability
    — the fraction of trait variation attributable to additive genes that offspring actually inherit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> A dairy herd averages 8,000 litres per lactation. You select the
    top cows, which average 9,000 L, so S = 1,000 L. Milk yield has h² ≈ 0.3, so the predicted
    response is R = 0.3 × 1,000 = 300 L in the next generation. Notice what the equation buys and
    costs: most of the selected cows' superiority was environment (better forage, better parlour), and
    heritability correctly discounts it. Progress compounds, though — gain every generation — and the
    rate per <em>year</em> is R divided by the generation interval, which is why species that breed
    young (poultry, ~1 year) improved so much faster than slow-replacing ones. UK dairy yields roughly
    doubled since the 1970s almost entirely through genetics plus better feeding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two systematic hazards follow. <strong>Inbreeding depression</strong>: related matings unmask
    deleterious recessives, costing fitness — the reason breed books cap inbreeding coefficients and
    why cheetahs and dog breeds are textbook casualties. <strong>Heterosis (hybrid vigour)</strong>:
    cross two inbred lines and the F1 often outperforms both — hybrid maize yields 15–20% above
    open-pollinated varieties, which is why virtually all the maize acreage of the Americas is hybrid.
    The molecular basis of heterosis is still genuinely unsettled (dominance complementation, over-dominance
    and epigenetic explanations all have evidence), making it one of the oldest open questions in
    applied genetics.
  </p>

  <h2>Marker-assisted and genomic selection</h2>
  <p>
    Classical breeding selects on phenotype, which is slow, expensive, and blind for traits you cannot
    see until harvest or until disease strikes. DNA markers changed the unit of selection. In
    <strong>marker-assisted selection (MAS)</strong>, a marker reliably linked to a gene lets you pick
    carriers as seedlings — decisive for recessive traits and for the backcrossing example above.
    <strong>Genomic selection</strong> (proposed by Meuwissen and colleagues in 2001, routine in dairy
    since ~2009) goes further: with tens of thousands of SNPs, a statistical model distributes a
    young animal's genome across the trait and estimates its breeding value at birth, before a single
    litre of milk exists. Accuracy around 0.5–0.7 versus ~0.3 from old pedigree records roughly halved
    the dairy generation interval, because bull calves no longer need five years of daughter
    progeny-testing. Genomic selection is now the quiet engine behind most measurable livestock and
    crop progress.
  </p>

  <h2>Transgenics: moving genes across species</h2>
  <p>
    Wide crosses hit a species barrier; recombinant DNA ignores it. The dominant workhorse is
    <em>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</em>, a soil bacterium that naturally transfers DNA into plant
    genomes — biologists disarmed its tumour genes and loaded cargo instead. The two traits that
    dominate global acreage:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bt insect resistance.</strong> Genes from <em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em> encode Cry
    proteins that are protoxins: harmless until dissolved and cleaved in the alkaline midgut of certain
    insects, where they bind specific receptors and punch holes in gut cells. Mammalian guts are acidic
    and receptor-mismatched, which is why Bt sprays were permitted in organic farming long before Bt
    crops existed. Field results: the Klümper and Qaim meta-analysis of 147 studies found insecticide
    use down about 37% and yields up about 22% on average for GM crops, with Bt cotton in India and
    China roughly halving spray regimes.</li>
    <li><strong>Herbicide tolerance.</strong> A glyphosate-insensitive version of the EPSPS enzyme
    (from <em>Agrobacterium</em> strain CP4) lets the crop survive blanket spraying. Agronomically
    transformative; ecologically contested, chiefly through the documented evolution of glyphosate-resistant
    weeds — an evolutionary consequence of selection pressure, not of the transgene itself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The rescue case worth remembering: Hawaii's papaya industry was being annihilated by papaya
    ringspot virus; the transgenic 'Rainbow' papaya (1998) expressed a viral coat protein and saved
    it — plant vaccination, in effect. Vitamin-A 'Golden Rice' (beta-carotene in the endosperm) finally
    received commercial approval in the Philippines in 2021, twenty-two years after publication,
    which is itself a datum about regulatory timelines.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "GMO" is not one technology with one risk profile — it is a legal
    category spanning a virus-resistant papaya and a herbicide-tolerant soybean with nothing in common
    biologically. Safety assessment is event-by-event: what protein, expressed where, digested how,
    with what allergenicity profile. Equally misleading is the claim that GM DNA behaves differently
    in the gut: like all DNA in food, it is degraded to nucleotides long before it could reach a
    consumer's genome.
  </p>

  <h2>CRISPR: editing within the species</h2>
  <p>
    Genome editing collapses the backcross-and-wait problem. A guide RNA walks Cas9 to any 20-base
    address in the genome and cuts there; the cell's own repair then produces the change. Three
    outcomes matter to breeders: <strong>SDN-1</strong> — the break is repaired error-prone (non-homologous
    end joining), knocking out a gene with a small deletion; <strong>SDN-2</strong> — a repair template
    directs a specific few-base change; <strong>SDN-3</strong> — a whole gene is inserted. The first
    two need no foreign DNA at all: an SDN-1 edit can be genetically indistinguishable from a mutant
    found in a breeding collection, because natural mutants are mostly deletions and point mutations too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deployed and pipeline examples: waxy (high-amylopectin) maize and rice created by knocking out
    granule-bound starch synthase; hornless (POLLED-locus) dairy cattle, where editing copies a
    naturally occurring allele into elite Holstein genetics; rice with edited <em>SWEET</em> gene
    promoters that bacterial blight can no longer hijack — a resistance that took a decade of
    conventional breeding to approximate. The deep advantage is <strong>absence of linkage drag</strong>:
    introgressing a trait by crossing drags along megabases of flanking donor DNA that must be
    recombined away over years; editing changes only the target bases and leaves everything else intact.
  </p>

  <h2>Who decides: the governance fork</h2>
  <p>
    Regulation bifurcated. The EU's Court of Justice (2018) ruled that organisms from mutagenesis
    techniques like CRISPR count as GMOs — process-based regulation, which in practice locks European
    breeders out of editing. The US, Argentina, Japan and (post-2023) the UK regulate the product:
    if an edit could have arisen naturally, it escapes GMO status. Two consequences follow. First,
    identical edited crops are "conventional" in Buenos Aires and "GMO" in Brussels, so export markets,
    not science, set breeding strategy. Second, the burden lands on precisely the crops that need it:
    orphan crops (cassava, millets, yam) with thin commercial markets are the ones where climate
    resilience is most urgent and public breeding budgets are weakest.
  </p>

  <h2>The conservation layer: gene banks and landraces</h2>
  <p>
    Breeding's raw material is variation, and variation is stored: landraces (farmer-selected
    local populations), wild relatives and elite lines held in gene banks — the Svalbard Global
    Seed Vault is the backup's backup of roughly 1,700 national and international collections
    holding over seven million accessions. The economics are sobering: wild relatives of staple
    crops carry heat, drought and disease tolerance that breeding programmes increasingly need,
    yet many collections are under-characterised and under-funded — the pre-breeding missions
    that hunt useful alleles in the "exotics" (CIMMYT and ICARDA's wheat and maize screens)
    are among applied agriculture's best-returning investments, and historically slow ones.
    Editing interacts with conservation directly: once a useful allele is known, it can be
    edited into an elite background without the linkage drag of crossing — potentially
    de-risking the bottleneck that has kept landrace diversity locked in storage while the
    climate deadline advances.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The quantitative layer.</strong> Real programmes select several traits at once, which
    turns R = h²S into index selection: weight traits by economic value, estimate breeding values
    multivariately, and accept a trade-off because genetic correlations make some traits antagonistic
    (selecting leaner pigs can raise sow mortality). The correlated response equation R<sub>y</sub> =
    i × h<sub>x</sub> × h<sub>y</sub> × r<sub>A</sub> × σ<sub>Py</sub> formalises the bargain.
    <strong>The rate race.</strong> To hold yields against 2 °C of warming, models suggest cereals
    need roughly 1–1.5% genetic gain per year — above what most breeding programmes deliver — which
    motivates speed breeding (extended photoperiods compressing 3 generations of wheat per year to 6),
    doubled haploids (instant homozygosity), and prediction-driven crossing.
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Genotype-by-environment interaction still defeats yield prediction:
    a variety's performance is not a stable property but a phenotype × environment surface, and
    machine-learning G×E models are the current frontier. Editing polyploids (wheat has six chromosome
    sets) means cutting every homoeologous copy simultaneously. And the off-target question has quietly
    inverted: with whole-genome sequencing, edited lines are now among the most thoroughly characterised
    varieties ever released — the uncertainty has shifted from the technique to the ecology, where
    resistance evolution and gene flow remain genuinely unsolved.
  </p>

